Title: "Proof Techniques and Formal Arguments"
Id: "1bcff66d-8661-4a2b-b6fe-7643a0399ee4"


Text: "A mathematical proof is a finite, mechanically verifiable demonstration
       that a target proposition follows inescapably from accepted axioms,
       definitions, and previously established theorems through sound inference
       rules. By Gödel's Completeness Theorem (1929), the syntactic notion of
       formal provability coincides with semantic consequence in first-order
       logic: a sentence is provable if and only if it is true in every possible
       interpretation."
Id: "a1fac498-1922-4ad6-9feb-6c914797e431"


Text: "First-order deduction extends propositional calculi by adding inference
       rules that govern universal and existential quantifiers. Universal
       Instantiation allows a universal assertion to be instantiated to any
       valid object term, while Universal Generalization allows an assertion
       verified for an arbitrary, unconstrained object to be generalized to all
       domain members."
Id: "e1832150-4dfa-4aa7-82be-45c15f6b4945"


[\rule.universal.instantiation]
Axiom:
given:
. phi is \\statement
. x is \fol.variable
. t is \fol.term
. D is \set
where:
. \fol.for.all{x}:in{D}{phi}
then:
. \fol.substitution{t}:for{x}:in{phi}
Documented:
. called: "Universal Instantiation (UI)"
. written: "\frac{\forall x \in D, \varphi(x)}{\varphi(t)}"
. description: "The inference rule allowing deduction of \varphi(t) for any
                substitutable term t from universal premise \forall x \in D,
                \varphi(x)."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{112}
Id: "507d82be-1548-4c89-a8ac-553f1304ef98"


[\rule.universal.generalization]
Axiom:
given:
. phi is \\statement
. y is \fol.variable
. D is \set
where:
. phi
then:
. \fol.for.all{y}:in{D}{phi}
Documented:
. called: "Universal Generalization (UG)"
. written: "\frac{\varphi(y)}{\forall x \in D, \varphi(x)}"
. description: "The inference rule permitting universal generalization \forall x
                \in D, \varphi(x) when \varphi(y) has been demonstrated for an
                arbitrary object y unconstrained by hypotheses."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{117}
Id: "d150a8be-978d-443f-92c0-aacc6bb05022"


Text: "Dual to the universal rules are the existential quantifier rules.
       Existential Generalization allows deducing that some element satisfies a
       property whenever an explicit witness is produced. Existential
       Instantiation allows introducing a fresh dummy constant to denote an
       unnamed witness guaranteed by an existential claim."
Id: "cd0b93a0-4de2-4145-bf45-632357bf3316"


[\rule.existential.generalization]
Axiom:
given:
. phi is \\statement
. x is \fol.variable
. t is \fol.term
. D is \set
where:
. \fol.substitution{t}:for{x}:in{phi}
then:
. \fol.exists{x}:in{D}{phi}
Documented:
. called: "Existential Generalization (EG)"
. written: "\frac{\varphi(t)}{\exists x \in D, \varphi(x)}"
. description: "The inference rule allowing deduction of existential claim
                \exists x \in D, \varphi(x) from an explicit witness \varphi(t)."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{118}
Id: "92313939-a286-4583-99ce-207adf0f9123"


[\rule.existential.instantiation]
Axiom:
given:
. phi is \\statement
. x is \fol.variable
. c is \fol.term
. D is \set
where:
. \fol.exists{x}:in{D}{phi}
then:
. \fol.substitution{c}:for{x}:in{phi}
Documented:
. called: "Existential Instantiation (EI)"
. written: "\frac{\exists x \in D, \varphi(x)}{\varphi(c)}"
. description: "The inference rule introducing a fresh witness constant c
                satisfying \varphi(c) from existential assertion \exists x \in
                D, \varphi(x)."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{120}
Id: "16121138-a3a2-46d7-b2f5-639dea55ac6b"


Text: "In first-order logic with equality, the identity symbol = is governed by
       equality axioms ensuring that equality is an equivalence relation and
       that equal terms are interchangeable across all formulas (Leibniz's
       principle of the indiscernibility of identicals)."
Id: "8404c8cf-a3d6-498d-8bd9-99cafe23190e"


[\fol.equality.reflexive]
Axiom:
given: x is \fol.term
then: (x = x)
Documented:
. called: "reflexivity of equality"
. written: "\forall x\,(x = x)"
. description: "Every object is identical to itself."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{94}
Id: "445ddb1f-5429-4a19-bcd3-df6c67ab542a"


[\fol.equality.congruence]
Axiom:
given:
. x is \fol.variable
. y is \fol.term
. phi is \\statement
where:
. x = y
. phi
then:
. \fol.substitution{y}:for{x}:in{phi}
Documented:
. called: "congruence / substitution of equals"
. written: "x = y \to (\varphi(x) \leftrightarrow \varphi(y))"
. description: "Equal terms may be substituted for each other in any formula
                without changing its truth value."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{94}
Id: "9bc137c3-af5d-4f32-8adf-bdfa77341276"


Text: "Beyond quantifier rules, working mathematicians rely on several canonical
       methods of proof tailored to the structural architecture of the
       proposition under examination: direct proof, proof by contraposition,
       proof by contradiction, counterexamples, and proof by cases."
Id: "cbd519b2-ea92-403e-92f6-6e2c1ef631bf"


Text: "A direct proof demonstrates a conditional proposition P \implies Q by
       assuming premise P as a local hypothesis and deducing a valid deductive
       chain culminating in Q."
Id: "3a03b640-90c3-4fdc-87fa-26698bbe288a"


[\proof.contraposition]
Theorem:
given: P, Q is \\statement
then: (P \.implies./ Q) \.iff./ ((\not{Q}) \.implies./ (\not{P}))
Documented:
. called: "proof by contraposition"
. written: "(P \implies Q) \iff (\neg Q \implies \neg P)"
. description: "The logical equivalence justifying proof of conditional P
                \implies Q by establishing its contrapositive \neg Q \implies
                \neg P."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{27}
Id: "d2301427-4df9-46e7-9e14-74735a8a2b9f"


[\proof.contradiction]
Theorem:
given: P is \\statement
then: (P is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "proof by contradiction (reductio ad absurdum)"
. written: "(\neg P \implies \bot) \implies P"
. description: "The classical deductive principle establishing P by showing that
                assuming its negation \neg P yields a formal logical
                contradiction."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{28}
Id: "3a45c656-83dc-4700-a4d9-88c9ca76468e"


Text: "To refute a universal proposition \forall x \in D, P(x), one is not
       required to show that P(x) is universally false; it is sufficient to
       exhibit a single concrete element c \in D—termed a counterexample—for
       which P(c) fails."
Id: "93f1ed4c-c32f-41df-9a25-b56e5c23e3b5"


[\proof.by.cases]
Theorem:
given: P1, P2, Q is \\statement
where:
. P1 \.or./ P2
. P1 \.implies./ Q
. P2 \.implies./ Q
then: Q
Documented:
. called: "proof by cases (disjunction elimination)"
. written: "((P_1 \lor P_2) \land (P_1 \implies Q) \land (P_2 \implies Q))
            \implies Q"
. description: "Establishing conclusion Q by verifying it across exhaustive
                alternative cases."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{28}
Id: "2f63c904-bc17-4054-bc21-9c839c42e909"


Text: "When asserting propositions quantified over the natural numbers
       \mathbb{N}, the Principle of Mathematical Induction serves as the supreme
       proof technique: verifying a property for zero and establishing that its
       truth is preserved under the successor operation guarantees universal
       truth across \mathbb{N}."
Id: "04cfc8e1-90af-4a39-81bd-e53773c0a26e"


[\proof.mathematical.induction]
Theorem:
given: P(n_) is (_ is \natural) -> (_ is \\statement)
where:
. P(\natural.zero)
. forAll: n is \natural
  where: P(n)
  then: P(\natural.successor:of{n})
then:
. forAll: n is \natural
  then: P(n)
Documented:
. called: "principle of mathematical induction"
. written: "P(0) \land (\forall n \in \mathbb{N}\,(P(n) \implies P(n+1)))
            \implies \forall n \in \mathbb{N}\,P(n)"
. description: "The foundational induction theorem stating that a property
                holding at zero and preserved under successors holds for all
                natural numbers."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{67}
Id: "3e6323da-c7b4-428e-a71f-144a2b90dfe4"


Text: "Sound mathematical demonstration demands vigilant avoidance of logical
       fallacies. Common pitfalls include formal fallacies in conditional
       reasoning (such as affirming the consequent and denying the antecedent)
       and quantifier fallacies in predicate logic.

       The most insidious quantifier error is the illicit quantifier swap:
       confusing \forall x\,\exists y\,P(x, y) with \exists y\,\forall x\,P(x,
       y). The assertion that every continuous function on [a, b] is bounded
       (pointwise) does not imply that a single bound bounds every continuous
       function simultaneously."
Id: "2a86eba0-8b33-4be3-a487-b0368e635a39"


[\fallacy.affirming.consequent]
Theorem:
given: P, Q is \\statement
then: (P is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "fallacy of affirming the consequent"
. written: "((P \implies Q) \land Q) \not\implies P"
. description: "The invalid formal inference of concluding antecedent P merely
                from conditional P \implies Q and consequent Q."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{34}
Id: "4f73c3b0-5e90-4bba-a5c5-33749971f94f"


[\fallacy.quantifier.swap]
Theorem:
given: P is \\statement
then: (P is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "fallacy of the illicit quantifier swap"
. written: "(\forall x\,\exists y\,P(x, y)) \not\implies (\exists y\,\forall
            x\,P(x, y))"
. description: "The fallacy of interchanging universal and existential
                quantifiers, conflating local dependence with global uniformity."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{92}
Id: "ea4bcfa5-9a88-4b66-925c-2009e16c0e77"
